A program always converts temperature using the fixed formula $F = \frac{9}{5}C+32$, regardless of any data it is given. Is this program an example of Machine Learning?

\textbf{Solution:}
The program applies the same fixed rule every time and never changes its behaviour based on experience. There is no data-driven improvement in performance.\\
\boxed{\text{No, this is traditional programming, not Machine Learning}}

A program studies thousands of past house sale records (size, location, price) and uses the patterns found in them to predict the price of a new house; its predictions get more accurate as more sale records are added. Is this program an example of Machine Learning?

\textbf{Solution:}
The program has an experience $E$ (past sale records), a task $T$ (predicting price), and a performance measure $P$ (prediction accuracy), and $P$ improves as $E$ grows.
\boxed{\text{Yes, this satisfies the formal definition of Machine Learning}}

An email program starts by flagging very few emails as spam. Every time a user manually marks an email as spam or not-spam, the program updates itself, and over time it correctly flags spam more often. Is this program an example of Machine Learning?

\textbf{Solution:}
The program's experience $E$ is the set of user-marked emails, its task $T$ is classifying new emails, and its performance $P$ (fraction of emails correctly classified) improves as $E$ grows.
\boxed{\text{Yes, this is Machine Learning}}

\textbf{Traditional Programming vs Machine Learning:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item \textbf{Traditional programming:} a human writes explicit rules; the same input always produces the same output through the same fixed logic (Example 1.1)
\item \textbf{Machine Learning:} the program is shown data (experience) and learns the rules or patterns itself; performance improves as more data is seen (Examples 1.2, 1.3)
\item The key test is always the same: is there an experience $E$ that, when increased, improves a measurable performance $P$ on a task $T$?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Well-Posed Learning Problems}

A program is being built to recognize hand-written digits (0 to 9) from scanned images, and it is given a large collection of digit images that are already labeled with the correct digit. Identify $T$, $P$, and $E$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
T &: \text{recognizing and classifying hand-written digits in new images} \\
P &: \text{percentage of digits correctly classified} \\
E &: \text{a collection of digit images with known correct labels}
\end{align*}
\boxed{T,P,E \text{ identified as above}}

A program is being built to play the game of checkers, and it improves by playing many practice games against itself and recording which moves led to wins. Identify $T$, $P$, and $E$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
T &: \text{choosing which move to play in a game of checkers} \\
P &: \text{percentage of games won against opponents} \\
E &: \text{a large number of self-played practice games}
\end{align*}
\boxed{T,P,E \text{ identified as above}}

A chatbot is being built to answer customer questions, and it is given a large set of past conversations along with a rating (helpful or not helpful) that customers gave to each chatbot reply. Identify $T$, $P$, and $E$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
T &: \text{generating a reply to a customer's question} \\
P &: \text{percentage of replies rated helpful by customers} \\
E &: \text{past conversations together with their helpfulness ratings}
\end{align*}
\boxed{T,P,E \text{ identified as above}}

\textbf{Quick Checklist for T P E:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item $T$ is always an \textbf{action or decision} the program performs (classify, predict, choose a move, generate a reply)
\item $P$ is always something you could actually \textbf{count or measure} (a percentage, an error rate, a score)
\item $E$ is always the \textbf{raw material} the program is trained on (labeled data, self-play games, past interactions)
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Types of Machine Learning}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item \textbf{Supervised Learning:} trained on examples that already have the correct output (label) attached; the goal is to predict the output for new, unseen inputs \hfill\textit{(labeled data, Chapter 2)}
\item \textbf{Unsupervised Learning:} trained on examples with no labels at all; the goal is to discover natural structure or groupings in the data \hfill\textit{(no labels, Chapter 3)}
\item \textbf{Reinforcement Learning:} an agent learns by interacting with an environment and receiving reward signals for its actions, rather than being shown correct answers directly \hfill\textit{(reward-driven, Chapter 4)}
\item \textbf{Semi-Supervised Learning:} trained on a small labeled set together with a larger unlabeled set, combining ideas from both supervised and unsupervised learning \hfill\textit{(partial labels, Chapter 4)}
\end{enumerate}

