A dataset contains 500 emails, and every single email is already marked Spam or NotSpam. A program is trained on this dataset to classify future emails. Which type of Machine Learning is this?

\textbf{Solution:}
Every example has a known label (Spam or NotSpam) attached, and the program is trained directly on these input-output pairs.
\boxed{\text{Supervised Learning}}

A store has 10,000 customer purchase records with no labels of any kind, and wants to discover natural groups of customers with similar buying habits. Which type of Machine Learning is this?

\textbf{Solution:}
There are no labels anywhere in the data; the goal is purely to discover structure (groups) in the data itself.
\boxed{\text{Unsupervised Learning}}

A robot arm learns to stack blocks by trying different movements; it receives a positive reward when a block is stacked successfully and a negative reward when a block falls, with no example of the "correct" movement ever given directly. Which type of Machine Learning is this?

\textbf{Solution:}
The robot learns purely through trial-and-error interaction with its environment, guided only by reward and penalty signals, not by labeled examples.
\boxed{\text{Reinforcement Learning}}

Let $h = \langle \text{Sunny}, \text{Warm} \rangle$ over attributes $\langle \text{Sky}, \text{AirTemp} \rangle$. Does the instance $x=(\text{Sunny},\text{Warm})$ satisfy $h$? Does $x=(\text{Rainy},\text{Warm})$ satisfy $h$?

\textbf{Solution:}
For $x=(\text{Sunny},\text{Warm})$: Sky matches (Sunny = Sunny) and AirTemp matches (Warm = Warm).
\boxed{x=(\text{Sunny},\text{Warm}) \text{ satisfies } h}
For $x=(\text{Rainy},\text{Warm})$: Sky does not match (Rainy $\ne$ Sunny).
\boxed{x=(\text{Rainy},\text{Warm}) \text{ does not satisfy } h}

Let $h = \langle \text{Sunny}, ? \rangle$. Does $x=(\text{Sunny},\text{Cold})$ satisfy $h$? Does $x=(\text{Rainy},\text{Cold})$ satisfy $h$?

\textbf{Solution:}
For $x=(\text{Sunny},\text{Cold})$: Sky matches (Sunny = Sunny); AirTemp constraint is "?", so any value is accepted.
\boxed{x=(\text{Sunny},\text{Cold}) \text{ satisfies } h}
For $x=(\text{Rainy},\text{Cold})$: Sky does not match (Rainy $\ne$ Sunny), so the "?" on AirTemp does not matter.
\boxed{x=(\text{Rainy},\text{Cold}) \text{ does not satisfy } h}

Let $h_1 = \langle ?, ? \rangle$ and $h_2 = \langle \varnothing, \varnothing \rangle$. Does $x=(\text{Rainy},\text{Cold})$ satisfy $h_1$? Does it satisfy $h_2$?

\textbf{Solution:}
For $h_1 = \langle ?, ? \rangle$: every attribute constraint is "?", so every instance is accepted.
\boxed{x \text{ satisfies } h_1 \text{ (in fact every possible instance satisfies } h_1\text{)}}
For $h_2 = \langle \varnothing, \varnothing \rangle$: the constraint $\varnothing$ rejects every value.
\boxed{x \text{ does not satisfy } h_2 \text{ (no instance ever satisfies } h_2\text{)}}

\textbf{Reading Hypothesis Constraints:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item A \textbf{specific value} constraint accepts only instances matching that exact value
\item \textbf{"?"} accepts every value of that attribute --- the least restrictive constraint
\item \textbf{"$\varnothing$"} accepts no value of that attribute --- the most restrictive constraint, making the whole hypothesis reject everything
\item $h = \langle ?, ?, \ldots, ? \rangle$ is the \textbf{most general} hypothesis (accepts every instance); $h = \langle \varnothing, \varnothing, \ldots, \varnothing \rangle$ is the \textbf{most specific} hypothesis (accepts no instance)
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{General-to-Specific Ordering of Hypotheses}

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item The relation $\ge_g$ is a \textbf{partial order}: not every pair of hypotheses is comparable \hfill\textit{(some pairs are incomparable)}
\item $\langle ?,?,\ldots,? \rangle$ is more general than (or equal to) every hypothesis in $H$ \hfill\textit{(top of the ordering)}
\item $\langle \varnothing,\varnothing,\ldots,\varnothing \rangle$ is more specific than (or equal to) every hypothesis in $H$ \hfill\textit{(bottom of the ordering)}
\item Replacing a "?" with a specific value, or a specific value with "$\varnothing$", always produces a \textbf{more specific} hypothesis \hfill\textit{(specialization step)}
\end{enumerate}

