Let $h_1 = \langle \text{Sunny}, ? \rangle$ and $h_2 = \langle \text{Sunny}, \text{Warm} \rangle$, over $\text{Sky}\in\{\text{Sunny},\text{Rainy}\}$ and $\text{AirTemp}\in\{\text{Warm},\text{Cold}\}$. Determine whether $h_1 \ge_g h_2$.

\textbf{Solution:}
List instances satisfying each hypothesis.
\begin{align*}
\text{Instances satisfying } h_2 &= \{(\text{Sunny},\text{Warm})\} \\
\text{Instances satisfying } h_1 &= \{(\text{Sunny},\text{Warm}),\ (\text{Sunny},\text{Cold})\}
\end{align*}
Every instance satisfying $h_2$ also satisfies $h_1$.
\boxed{h_1 \ge_g h_2 \quad (\text{in fact } h_1 >_g h_2)}

Let $h_1 = \langle ?, \text{Warm} \rangle$ and $h_2 = \langle \text{Sunny}, \text{Warm} \rangle$. Determine whether $h_1 \ge_g h_2$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{Instances satisfying } h_2 &= \{(\text{Sunny},\text{Warm})\} \\
\text{Instances satisfying } h_1 &= \{(\text{Sunny},\text{Warm}),\ (\text{Rainy},\text{Warm})\}
\end{align*}
Every instance satisfying $h_2$ also satisfies $h_1$.
\boxed{h_1 \ge_g h_2 \quad (\text{in fact } h_1 >_g h_2)}

Let $h_1 = \langle \text{Sunny}, ? \rangle$ and $h_2 = \langle ?, \text{Warm} \rangle$. Determine whether $h_1 \ge_g h_2$ or $h_2 \ge_g h_1$.

\textbf{Solution:}
\begin{align*}
\text{Instances satisfying } h_1 &= \{(\text{Sunny},\text{Warm}),\ (\text{Sunny},\text{Cold})\} \\
\text{Instances satisfying } h_2 &= \{(\text{Sunny},\text{Warm}),\ (\text{Rainy},\text{Warm})\}
\end{align*}
Neither set contains the other: $(\text{Sunny},\text{Cold})$ satisfies $h_1$ but not $h_2$, and $(\text{Rainy},\text{Warm})$ satisfies $h_2$ but not $h_1$.
\boxed{h_1 \text{ and } h_2 \text{ are incomparable --- neither is more general than the other}}
This confirms Property 1 of the theorembox above: the ordering is only a \textit{partial} order, since not every pair of hypotheses can be compared. \checkmark

\textbf{How to Compare Two Hypotheses:}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=2em,itemsep=4pt]
\item List (or imagine) the instances each hypothesis accepts
\item If every instance accepted by $h_2$ is also accepted by $h_1$, then $h_1 \ge_g h_2$
\item If neither hypothesis's accepted set contains the other, the two hypotheses are \textbf{incomparable}
\item This ordering is exactly what guides a search through hypothesis space: moving up means generalizing, moving down means specializing
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The Find-S Algorithm}

