\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
\# & Taste & Size & Target \\
\midrule
1 & Sweet & Big & No \\
2 & Sour & Small & Yes \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Trace the Candidate Elimination Algorithm and list every hypothesis in the final version space.

\textbf{Solution:}
\[
S_0 = \{\langle \varnothing,\varnothing \rangle\}, \qquad G_0 = \{\langle ?,? \rangle\}
\]
\textbf{Example 1 (Sweet, Big, No) --- negative:} $S_0$ already rejects everything (unchanged). Specialize $G_0$ minimally.
\begin{align*}
\text{Taste} &\to \text{Sour}: \langle \text{Sour},? \rangle \\
\text{Size} &\to \text{Small}: \langle ?,\text{Small} \rangle
\end{align*}
(No positive example has been seen yet, so both specializations are valid.)
\[
S_1 = \{\langle \varnothing,\varnothing \rangle\}, \qquad G_1 = \{\langle \text{Sour},? \rangle,\ \langle ?,\text{Small} \rangle\}
\]
\textbf{Example 2 (Sour, Small, Yes) --- positive:} generalize $S_1$ minimally to cover it.
\[
S_2 = \{\langle \text{Sour},\text{Small} \rangle\}
\]
Check each member of $G_1$: both accept (Sour,Small), so both are kept.
\[
G_2 = \{\langle \text{Sour},? \rangle,\ \langle ?,\text{Small} \rangle\}
\]
\boxed{\text{Final: } S = \{\langle \text{Sour},\text{Small} \rangle\}, \quad G = \{\langle \text{Sour},? \rangle,\ \langle ?,\text{Small} \rangle\}}
Every hypothesis $h$ between $S$ and some member of $G$ belongs to the version space:
\[
\text{Version Space} = \big\{\ \langle \text{Sour},\text{Small} \rangle,\ \ \langle \text{Sour},? \rangle,\ \ \langle ?,\text{Small} \rangle\ \big\}
\]

